/* The header for this test is at the BOTTOM of this file, below the script.
   It is there because a constant inside a leading comment can swallow the
   rest of the file in the engine - see RMSTEST_56 and the directory README.
   Nothing below the script can do any harm. */

<PLAYER_SETUP>
  random_placement

<LAND_GENERATION>
  base_terrain GRASS

  create_land
  {
    terrain_type GRASS
    base_size 4
    number_of_tiles 300
    zone 2
    land_position 20 20
  }
  create_land
  {
    terrain_type DIRT
    base_size 4
    number_of_tiles 300
    zone 3
    land_position 80 50
  }
  create_land
  {
    terrain_type GRASS2
    base_size 4
    number_of_tiles 300
    zone 4
    land_position 20 80
  }

<ELEVATION_GENERATION>


<TERRAIN_GENERATION>


<OBJECTS_GENERATION>


<CONNECTION_GENERATION>

  create_connect_all_lands
  {
    replace_terrain GRASS ROAD
    replace_terrain GRASS2 ROAD
    replace_terrain DIRT ROAD
  }

/*
================================================================================
RMSTEST_75b - control for RMSTEST_75a. Identical map, no terrain_cost line
at all, and DIRT is itself declared replaceable so a road crossing B is
visible instead of merely absent-by-omission.
Feeds: docs/known-issues.md BUG-029, preview-design.md Sec.6.5

Byte-for-byte the same three lands, same positions, same zones as 75a. The
only difference is this command drops "terrain_cost DIRT 0" and adds
"replace_terrain DIRT ROAD" in its place, so that with DIRT at its
ordinary default cost of 1, all three pairs should connect freely and a
road should be able to run straight through B's body if the shortest path
calls for it.

WHY THIS CONTROL MATTERS AND ISN'T REDUNDANT WITH "not blocked" IN 75a
75a's own "not blocked" outcome already shows what an unblocked B looks
like in principle, but it is read off the SAME command that is testing the
hypothesis - if something in the setup (map layout, zone assignment,
A* tie-breaking) happened to route every path AWAY from B regardless of
cost, 75a could read as "blocked" for a reason that has nothing to do with
terrain_cost. This script is the same geometry with terrain_cost's only
effect removed, so three roads (or at least a road touching every land)
here is what confirms the setup is capable of producing them at all.

PREDICTION
Three roads, or a connected road network touching every one of A, B and C.
ROAD tiles appear inside or immediately adjacent to DIRT's own bounding
box on every run.

GENERATE AT NORMAL (200), ANY PLAYER COUNT. THREE RUNS, matching 75a.

READ WITH
  python probe_scenario.py "<scenario>" --bbox DIRT
  python probe_scenario.py "<scenario>" --patches ROAD
  Confirm ROAD tiles sit inside or touching DIRT's bbox on every run. If
  they do not - if this control ALSO reads as "blocked" - 75a's result is
  uninterpretable and the geometry needs redesigning before trusting either
  script.
================================================================================
*/
